WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:07.921
- [Interviewer] All right, Asha.

00:00:07.921 --> 00:00:08.780
- Hi.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.840
- [Interviewer] Asha, where
are you from originally?

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.090
Where'd you grow up?

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:13.395
- [Asha] Where did I
grow up? Silver Lake, LA.

00:00:13.395 --> 00:00:14.228
- [Interviewer] LA?

00:00:14.228 --> 00:00:15.061
- [Asha] Yeah.

00:00:15.061 --> 00:00:16.954
- [Interviewer] And tell
me about your family.

00:00:16.954 --> 00:00:20.960
- [Asha] My father is
Peruvian and my mom's Korean.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:25.583
I was raised from my paternal
side by my grandmother,

00:00:26.666 --> 00:00:29.360
a long line of alcoholic.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:30.810
I don't know so much about drug addicts

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:32.250
but big time alcoholics.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:33.220
- [Interviewer] Is that why your parents

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:34.750
were out of the picture?

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.240
- No, my parents were out of the picture

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.600
because my mom wanted a
green card, not a daughter.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.770
She got stuck with a daughter
who she quickly abandoned

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.930
'cause she used to get
the shit beat out of her.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.000
So she left me with my dad who decided

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.260
that he would give me to his mom

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:55.203
and she raised me until I was 13.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:00:59.010
And then she decided
that she had done her job

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.830
and it's time for me to come
back to live with my dad.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:04.113
So I lasted 12 months and I moved out.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:07.785
- [Interviewer] And you
moved out of what age?

00:01:07.785 --> 00:01:08.618
- 14.

00:01:08.618 --> 00:01:09.576
- [Interviewer] 14.

00:01:09.576 --> 00:01:10.409
- And so-

00:01:10.409 --> 00:01:12.600
- [Interviewer] How
was life with your dad?

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.203
- Hard, you know, he
grew up very old school.

00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:20.560
The woman cooks, cleans,
does everything for the man.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.330
In this case, even though
I wasn't like a wife

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.430
I was still his daughter,

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:30.430
and his form of correcting
you was very violent.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:36.930
He would, you know, punch me,

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.210
he loved to punch me.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:43.570
And his favorite was like
throwing chairs at me.

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:48.223
So it wasn't exactly like
the greatest atmosphere.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.580
Like I said, I put up with it full time

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.073
12 months and I moved out.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:56.360
I was able to get a job with the city

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:00.650
painting murals in Silver
Lake, for the summer.

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:04.157
So earning my 4.15 an hour,
I was very proud and said,

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:06.236
"You know what? I don't
need to put up with your.

00:02:06.236 --> 00:02:08.070
I'm outta here."

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.890
I never went home.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:12.323
I had, you know,

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:18.380
crazy year being 14 and
ended up pregnant at 15.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.990
Had my daughter at 16, pregnant at 16,

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:24.713
had my son at 17,

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:31.870
by then I was very, very
addicted to crack cocaine.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.883
Was fortunate I didn't
use while I was pregnant,

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:43.883
but soon after my son was born,
I fell right back into it.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.520
'Cause that's how I
survived, I sold crack,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.007
I mean, that's what I knew how to do.

00:02:50.007 --> 00:02:54.370
And endless supply, so it was easy for me

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.803
to just fall into it.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:00.890
Long story short, when my
son was about, I don't know,

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:05.530
year and a half, he wanted me to carry him

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:08.280
and I pushed him away 'cause
I needed to go take a hit.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.060
And that was my bottom.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.903
I got sober and was sober
for 10 years, raised my kids.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.253
They are great amazing
people, not like me,

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.170
but you know how shit happens.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:25.820
Life goes on, right?

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.070
So we moved to Georgia
so I could give them

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.410
the lifestyle that they deserved.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.653
And I met my current husband there.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.250
He'd like, I'd mentioned outside,

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.450
he was in the military for eight years,

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.370
four of which I was with him.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:47.023
We went through one deployment
together to Afghanistan,

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:52.610
and he came back a very different person.

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:56.880
And I turned back into what I guess

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.173
I tried to get away from.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:03.730
And unfortunately I ended up
in jail for aggravated assault,

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:07.670
met the wrong girl and tried heroin.

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:10.373
Brought it home and just,

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:17.650
it was an endless insatiable like

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.500
stomach tiffy, you know.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:23.410
I didn't know what kind
of addicts my husband was.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:25.063
I knew what kind of addict I was.

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:30.290
And if it's in my face, I
can't say no, I have to do it.

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:35.290
So he'd make sure that
there was just enough

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.300
for me to fuck up that day
so I couldn't bitch at him.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:39.430
Woke up sick one day,

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:42.620
a couple of years later
we lost everything.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:45.800
So after 20 years of not selling dope

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.130
I went right back to selling it.

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:50.940
And I kept us off the
streets for about a year.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:55.613
We stayed in hotels and I
caught charges eventually.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.810
Ended up finding a rehab for couples

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:02.070
which is really unheard
of, we lasted a week.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:05.430
We got kicked out because
of our anger issues.

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:08.250
And the idea was since
he receives a pension

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.430
we'd go to Mexico and live
comfortably if anything,

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:13.680
and still be able to get high of course.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:17.320
But when we got here, we
needed about two weeks

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:20.550
till he got his paycheck
for us to cross into Mexico.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:24.690
While we were here, our IDs
and his bank card got stolen.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.993
So we got sucked into downtown.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.323
It's been very wild, you know.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:41.090
Good example, I recently had
to like leave the downtown area

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:45.880
and go to MacArthur because
some of the black sets

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:50.550
some of the males had pretty
much kept their eye on me

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:53.020
'cause I'm loyal to my husband.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.120
And I know how to make money by hustling,

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:58.403
selling whatever, not myself.

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:04.300
So they took, how do I put this?

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:12.230
Basically they had a meeting so to speak,

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:16.610
and said, "These are the
males from these sets."

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:18.980
That they were interested in making me

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:24.000
a new boss or a boss bitch.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.730
And I guess it's what it's called.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:30.230
And so I had a choice, I
could become what they wanted

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:32.180
and my husband could
come along for the ride

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:36.551
and not be hurt, or
they would just take me

00:06:36.551 --> 00:06:39.471
and put me in a tent
and do what they wanted.

00:06:39.471 --> 00:06:41.290
And my husband would get fucked off.

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:46.290
So we went to MacArthur
where there's my race

00:06:47.130 --> 00:06:49.300
and looked for, I guess, sanctuary there.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:54.300
It's worked out so far, but
it's never a dull moment, ever.

00:06:55.151 --> 00:06:58.800
(laughs) There's so many compromises

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:02.753
that you have to make out here.

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:05.790
- [Interviewer] Where are you living now?

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:10.790
- Across the street from
(indistinct) (laughs) on Alvarado

00:07:11.161 --> 00:07:12.570
- [Interviewer] At MacArthur Park?

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.250
- Yeah, in MacArthur Park.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.083
- [Interviewer] Did a tent?

00:07:15.083 --> 00:07:17.103
- Yeah, in a tent, so.

00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:18.160
- [Interviewer] With your husband though?

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:19.850
- Yes, with my husband.

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:22.123
That part's not so bad, believe it or not.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:25.970
It's everything that goes on
around it, that's kinda bad.

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:29.780
Although I do miss running
water (laughs), so.

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:32.043
- [Interviewer] How does
MacArthur Park compare Skid Row?

00:07:33.331 --> 00:07:35.710
- (sighs) It's the same,
but it's very different.

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.747
I mean, it's the same, you know you have-

00:07:40.271 --> 00:07:42.221
- [Interviewer] That is rundown and is-

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:46.270
- You know, downtown, isn't
what it used to be though.

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.380
They had all these facelifts,

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:50.290
and but Skid Row is still Skid Row,

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:51.870
and it's still, you know-

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:52.800
- [Interviewer] Downtown has improved,

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.520
but Skid Row is still-
- It's still Skid Row.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.770
- It is, and it probably always will be

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:03.020
until they decide to move the
entire homeless population

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:06.023
to a whole other area, which you know,

00:08:06.023 --> 00:08:08.823
with everything that's happening
right now could happen.

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:13.795
I think part of it is, you know,

00:08:13.795 --> 00:08:17.140
you come up seeing something
and it's all you know,

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:19.923
so why fix it if it's not broken?

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:24.280
'Cause nobody realizes just
how broken we all are out here.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:25.740
We don't realize, or maybe we do

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.960
and we choose not to see it.

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:32.960
So we get high or we
go do whatever mission

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:37.493
just to take our mind off
of what's really going on.

00:08:38.621 --> 00:08:42.210
And it gets old, but it's, you know,

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:44.350
today's adventure (laughs).

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:46.500
We're all a bunch of
big kids out here, man.

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.030
I mean, I know I am.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.990
I like step back and
see some of the things

00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:52.320
that like we do, and try to look at it

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:53.570
from an outsider's point of view,

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:55.130
and it's like, "Man, I'm 14 again,

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:58.200
I'm going on this little convoy

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.160
to find a place to go get high

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:01.980
where the cops won't find us."

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:03.440
Where this used to be pot now-

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:05.750
- [Interviewer] It's adults
living with no responsibility.

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:06.583
- Right.

00:09:06.583 --> 00:09:08.450
And there's such an allure to that,

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:12.340
but believe it or not there's
so many more rules out here.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:17.190
I mean, the real world doesn't
have shit on us out here

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:19.280
'cause you have to make
sure that you don't

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:22.420
say the wrong thing or look the wrong way.

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:23.940
- [Interviewer] Yeah, it's
a different set of rules.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:25.260
Very different but-

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:26.457
- That don't make sense all the time.

00:09:26.457 --> 00:09:27.290
- [Interviewer] Yeah.

00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:28.680
- It's kind of like math laws.

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:29.710
It's just always has been.

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:31.360
- [Interviewer] And it
changes block by block.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:32.330
- True.

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:33.830
But it's just the way it's always been.

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:38.190
So it always will be, you know,
there's again, why fix it?

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:42.250
And it allows those that
are strong to survive

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:43.780
and those that aren't to not.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.030
- [Interviewer] Do you
prefer this lifestyle

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:48.060
to one where you have a
job, and you pay bills,

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:49.460
and you have responsibilities?

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:50.640
- I don't know that I prefer it.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:53.290
I'm comfortable, it's familiar to me.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:54.963
It's what I grew up knowing.

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:00.250
Growing up with my grandmother, I mean,

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:02.580
you know, as an adult you
look back at your childhood

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:05.137
and whatnot and you
know in a way it's like,

00:10:05.137 --> 00:10:07.480
"How can I not be what I am today?

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:09.430
Which is how can I not hustle?"

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:11.170
I remember my grandmother sewing

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.460
money into my great-grandfather's suit

00:10:15.460 --> 00:10:18.500
to go to Peru, back home to Peru,

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:19.497
you know, shit like that.

00:10:19.497 --> 00:10:21.310
And you think back and you're
like, "Well, wait a minute,"

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:23.867
as an adult, "That's
not very legal" (laughs)

00:10:23.867 --> 00:10:26.240
"Weren't they supposed to report that?"

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:31.180
So it's just sometimes
things that we go through

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:34.760
lead us to a point in
life that we have no idea

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:36.593
what or where we're going.

00:10:38.462 --> 00:10:41.743
I'm just trying to keep
my head above water,

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:46.970
and it gets hard, it gets
old, but it's what I do.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:48.330
- [Interviewer] Do you
feel like you're following

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:49.573
in someone's footsteps?

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:51.913
- Not really.

00:10:53.663 --> 00:10:58.610
I mean, as far as like the
normal, well not normal,

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:03.127
but the average stereotype
of a female out here,

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:05.280
not even really that.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:07.600
- [Interviewer] But you
don't see any behaviors

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:09.680
in your parents that you're kind of-

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:11.370
- I mean, I'm kind of amoral,

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:15.213
like things that should not
be okay with me are, you know.

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:18.690
It's like, I don't really look down on

00:11:18.690 --> 00:11:21.530
maybe not paying your taxes
on time, things like that.

00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:23.490
You know, things like
that are kind of trivial,

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:26.250
but it's just relative.

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:30.120
It's just a different level,
I guess, than out here.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:35.030
It's like, it's you know,
it's okay to I guess

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:38.370
maybe screw on paying
the bill at the store.

00:11:38.370 --> 00:11:42.140
So we go in, some of us will
shoplift or what have you,

00:11:42.140 --> 00:11:44.780
and we know it's wrong, I know it's wrong

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:49.180
but I'll take the merchandise
if I need it, you know.

00:11:49.180 --> 00:11:51.450
It's whatever I have to do to survive.

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:54.110
- [Interviewer] Is the
cutting on your left arm

00:11:54.110 --> 00:11:56.680
from long ago or from recent?

00:11:56.680 --> 00:11:57.730
- No, I'm still a cutter.

00:11:57.730 --> 00:11:58.600
- [Interviewer] You're still a cutter?

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:02.900
- Yeah, I've done quite well
I'd say in the last year

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:04.520
I've only cut maybe once or twice,

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:06.360
and it wasn't very deep,

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:10.370
but I started when I was 14
just like any other addiction

00:12:11.946 --> 00:12:15.140
it had to get worse,
more stitches, deeper,

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:15.973
just to fix the same-

00:12:15.973 --> 00:12:17.580
- [Interviewer] Have you
ever thought you were

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:20.590
truly suicidal or is it just
like a call for attention?

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:24.090
- No, I mean, I've woken
up on life support before.

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:26.430
So, that sounds so dramatic,

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:28.720
you know on a ventilator (indistinct).

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:31.200
So that's also another reason why we,

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:35.854
we being me and my kids moved
to Georgia about 14 years ago.

00:12:35.854 --> 00:12:38.570
I'd had a really bad
episode, never really dealt

00:12:38.570 --> 00:12:42.663
with my depression and went on,

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:44.523
I guess, I don't know.

00:12:44.523 --> 00:12:45.680
I guess from what I was told

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:48.400
I started on a Friday
and woke up on a Monday.

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:51.230
It's a machine breathing for me,

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:55.690
and I'd had been really,
really just upset and depressed

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:57.963
overall about life.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:05.090
My great aunt, she was
the eldest of our elders

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:10.090
on my dad's side, had basically
turned her back on me.

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:11.520
And she's the only person I had had

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:13.560
who loved me unconditionally.

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:15.360
Unfortunately she's mentally ill

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:18.670
and it was my obligation
to have her committed

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:20.620
for the first time in her life.

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:23.507
So she stopped interacting with me

00:13:23.507 --> 00:13:25.750
and I couldn't take it (laughs).

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:29.260
So apparently I ODed

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:34.533
on a whole bunch of crap
pills and booze and whatnot.

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:38.003
But as far as the cutting's concerned,

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:41.100
you know it's in designs 'cause

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:43.090
I don't want my kids growing up knowing

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:45.120
that their mom was not okay.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:46.769
So I could lie to them and tell them,

00:13:46.769 --> 00:13:48.780
"Well, it's just a tattoo without ink."

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:50.460
'Cause you know, kids
are supposed to be kids.

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:51.320
They're not supposed to worry

00:13:51.320 --> 00:13:53.623
that their mom's not all right today.

00:13:54.890 --> 00:13:57.993
And I mean, it worked until it didn't.

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:02.273
And when it didn't, it
was really, really bad.

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:04.883
I got drunk,

00:14:07.997 --> 00:14:09.130
and ended up getting like

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:11.370
over a hundred stitches that night.

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:13.730
Unfortunately in the process of cutting,

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:16.770
I woke up my kids and they saw me being

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:19.740
pretty much carried out to my
car in a big old bloody mess.

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:22.720
And they'd never seen
that, they didn't know.

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:23.910
So I think that was a lot worse

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:26.340
than maybe as they were getting older,

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:27.217
sitting them down being like,

00:14:27.217 --> 00:14:30.090
"Hey, this is what's really going on."

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:33.200
But I didn't, I don't know.

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:36.540
I had an idea of what a good
parent was supposed to be

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:40.500
'cause I learned everything
to not do (laughs)

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:41.703
from my parents.

00:14:42.845 --> 00:14:47.630
And sometimes it works
and sometimes it doesn't.

00:14:50.785 --> 00:14:52.286
Any other questions?

00:14:52.286 --> 00:14:53.560
- [Interviewer] No, it's good.

00:14:53.560 --> 00:14:55.580
Asha, thank you so much
for talking with me and-

00:14:55.580 --> 00:14:56.413
- No problem.

00:14:56.413 --> 00:14:57.437
- [Interviewer] Good luck and hope you

00:14:57.437 --> 00:14:59.180
and your husband figure this out.

00:14:59.180 --> 00:15:01.700
- Oh we will, we've been
here over a year, so.

00:15:01.700 --> 00:15:03.174
- [Interviewer] Excellent, thank you.

00:15:03.174 --> 00:15:05.257
- [Asha] Cool, thank you.

